        <p>
          In Ayurveda, the year is divided into six seasons (Ritus). <strong class="text-text-primary">Karkidakam</strong> sits at the cusp of <em>Varsha Ritu</em> (the rainy season), a time when Vata, having accumulated through the dry summer, is now provoked, and Pitta begins to gather quietly in the gut. The body's <em>agni</em> (digestive fire) is at its weakest, the skin pores are open, and the channels of circulation are soft and pliable. This is precisely the physiological state that makes Panchakarma succeed: the toxins move easily, the medicines absorb deeply, and the tissues respond to nourishment with unusual speed.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <em>Ashtanga Hridayam</em> (Sutrasthana 3) and the <em>Charaka Samhita</em> both advise <em>Sodhana</em> (cleansing) and <em>Snehapanam</em> (internal oleation) during this window, followed by a measured Rasayana programme. Skipping this seasonal correction is, classically, one reason chronic disease builds up year after year: the body never gets a chance to reset.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-text-muted italic border border-brand-gold-dim bg-brand-gold/5 px-4 py-3 mt-6">
